    units: 428,
    text: "2026年，重庆奉节县青莲镇建设了一个“一老一小”服务联合体，把老人的日间照料和儿童的托育放在同一栋楼里。早上，居民可以先把老人安顿在按摩椅上休息，再把孩子交给志愿者辅导手工和剪纸，然后走进隔壁的工厂上班。\n\n这个联合体的出现，解决了不少家庭的现实困难。在此之前，镇上很多在工业园区上班的工人，因为家中老人需要照顾、孩子放学后无人看管，不得不放弃工作留在家中，家庭收入明显减少。当地民政部门在调研中发现，镇街工业园区工人中，八成以上被养老和托幼问题牵绊。联合体投用后，返乡创业园的招工率提升了29.1%，为210多名务工人员解除了后顾之忧。\n\n在服务内容上，联合体并非简单地把老人和孩子放在一起，而是根据各自需求安排了不同的活动。老人可以接受健康监测、参与书法和棋牌活动；孩子则由专人看护，开展阅读、手工和户外游戏。中午，老人和孩子可以在同一食堂就餐，饭菜根据年龄特点分别准备。\n\n这种做法正在更多地方推广。有社区把养老驿站重新规划，分出一半面积做托育中心，一条走廊贯通，老人和孩子一起做手工，老人手把手教孩子握笔、填色。相比“一老一小”分开托管，这种模式的家庭开销可以减少近三成。",
    reference: "In 2026, Qinglian Township in Fengjie County, Chongqing, built an integrated service center for the elderly and children, placing daytime care for seniors and childcare for young children in the same building. In the morning, residents can settle an elderly family member in a massage chair, hand their child over to volunteers for crafts and paper-cutting, and then walk to the factory next door to start work.\n\nThe center has solved real difficulties for many families. Before it opened, many workers employed in the township’s industrial park had to give up their jobs to stay home and care for elderly relatives or children who had no one to look after them after school, leading to a clear drop in household income. A survey by local civil affairs authorities found that more than 80 percent of workers in the township’s industrial park were held back by elderly care and childcare issues. After the center opened, the recruitment rate at the local business park rose by 29.1 percent, lifting the burden for more than 210 workers.\n\nIn terms of services, the center does not simply put the elderly and children in the same room. It arranges different activities based on their respective needs. Seniors can receive health checks and take part in calligraphy and board games. Children are looked after by dedicated staff and engage in reading, crafts and outdoor games. At noon, both groups can eat in the same canteen, with meals prepared according to their age-specific needs.\n\nThis approach is being adopted in more places. Some communities have replanned their elderly care stations, setting aside half the space for a childcare center. A corridor connects the two, where seniors and children do crafts together, with the elderly teaching children how to hold a brush and fill in colors. Compared with separate care arrangements, this model can reduce household costs by nearly 30 percent.",
