    text: "在湖南湘江新区一个居民小区里，一处600平方米的闲置空间经过改造后，变成了居民们口中的“第二个家”。走进这里，有人在长桌旁练字，有人在舞蹈室排练，还有人围坐在议事角商量下周邻里集市的事。\n\n改造前，这个小区面临不少治理难题：公共空间碎片化，居民需求各不相同，邻里之间缺乏互动。社区工作人员挨家挨户上门征求意见，发现青少年想要游乐阅读区，老年人想要休闲运动角，年轻人则希望有休憩空间。社区引入专业设计师，开了多轮邻里协商会，最终把单一闲置场地规划为邻里客厅、手工室、绘本坊等九个功能区。\n\n空间建好后，社区招募了15名热心公益、身怀特长的居民担任“社区主理人”。绘本达人带着孩子创作，瑜伽老师免费开课，宝妈组团开展亲子共读。22名居民自发组成志愿队伍，承担场地值守和环境保洁。每月28日被设为邻里议事日，社区工作人员、居民代表和物业人员围坐一堂，共商小区治理难题。\n\n这种前置化、面对面的共治模式效果明显：小区关于公共空间的投诉同比下降了62%。截至目前，“拾光集”已开展各类公益活动300余场。一位居民说：“这里是我们大家一起攒出来的家。”",
    reference: "In a residential community in Hunan Xiangjiang New Area, a 600-square-meter space that had long sat idle was renovated and turned into what residents now call their “second home.” Inside, some practice calligraphy at a long table, others rehearse in the dance room, and a few sit in the discussion corner planning next week’s neighborhood market.\n\nBefore the renovation, the community faced a number of governance challenges: fragmented public spaces, diverse and sometimes conflicting resident needs, and weak neighborly interaction. Community workers went door to door to collect opinions and found that young people wanted a play and reading area, seniors wanted a leisure and exercise corner, and younger adults hoped for a rest space. The community brought in professional designers and held multiple rounds of neighborhood consultations, eventually planning the single idle site into nine functional zones, including a neighborhood living room, a craft room and a picture-book workshop.\n\nOnce the space was built, the community recruited 15 residents with public spirit and special skills to serve as “community hosts.” A picture-book enthusiast led children in creative drawing, a yoga teacher offered free classes, and a group of mothers organized parent-child reading sessions. Twenty-two residents formed a volunteer team to handle venue duty and cleaning. The 28th of each month was designated as a neighborhood discussion day, when community workers, resident representatives and property staff sit down together to talk through governance issues.\n\nThis face-to-face, preventive approach to shared governance has produced clear results: complaints about public spaces in the community dropped by 62 percent year-on-year. To date, the space has hosted more than 300 public welfare activities. As one resident put it, “This is a home we built together.”",
